# Goal Tracker — Dockerized Multi-Container App

A simple goal tracking application containerized with Docker. Built as part of my self-taught DevOps learning journey.

## What This Project Does

Users can add personal goals with deadlines and view how many days remain for each goal. Data persists across container restarts.

## Tech Stack

| Component | Technology |
|-----------|------------|
| App | Python 3.12 |
| Database | PostgreSQL 18 |
| DB UI | Adminer |
| Orchestration | Docker Compose |
| Environment | Ubuntu Linux VM |

## Architecture

```
┌─────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐
│   app       │────▶│     db       │◀────│   adminer   │
│ (Python)    │     │ (PostgreSQL) │     │  port 8080  │
└─────────────┘     └──────┬───────┘     └─────────────┘
                           │
                    ┌──────▼───────┐
                    │ Docker Volume│
                    │ (persistent) │
                    └──────────────┘
```

## How to Run

**Prerequisites:** Docker and Docker Compose installed on a Linux machine.

```bash
# Clone the repo
git clone https://github.com/ajogiyat711-gif/goal-tracker
cd goal-tracker

# Start all containers
docker compose up --build

# In a second terminal — interact with the app
docker compose run app
```

Open `http://<your-ip>:8080` in your browser to access Adminer and view the database.

**Adminer login:**
- System: PostgreSQL
- Server: db
- Username: user
- Password: password
- Database: my_database

## Key Concepts Demonstrated

- Custom Dockerfile with multi-stage dependency installation
- Multi-container orchestration with docker-compose
- Container networking — app reaches db using service name as hostname
- Persistent storage with named Docker volumes
- Environment variable based configuration — no hardcoded credentials in code
- Separation of concerns — app, database, and UI as independent services

## What I Learned Building This

This wasn't just a follow-along tutorial. I wrote the Dockerfile and docker-compose from scratch, hit real errors, and debugged them myself — including a postgres:18 volume path change that required reading the official GitHub issue to understand.

